+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PCGco5432dKTnnExswrjrG","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 55 tests pass and my probe files are cleaned up (no source files modified). Here are my findings.\n\n## Review: Plugin Marketplace (`3f12e78`)\n\n### What was done well\n\nThe module boundaries are exactly as specced — zero cross-module imports, everything flows through `types.ts` interfaces, `types.ts` untouched. SQL is fully parameterized throughout (`instr(lower(col), ?)` rather than string-concatenated `LIKE`, which sidesteps both injection *and* `%`/`_` wildcard-injection — I verified `search('%')` and `search('100%')` behave as literal substring searches). The deps DFS is genuinely well-built: `onPath`/`finished` sets are correct, cycle canonicalization by rotation correctly dedups the same loop reached from different entry points while keeping genuinely distinct cycles separate (verified: two cycles sharing node `A` report as `[[\"A\",\"B\"],[\"A\",\"C\"]]`, not collapsed). Integration rollback restores the prior manifest on failure and removes on first-install failure — both paths verified working. Comments explain *why*, not *what*.\n\n---\n\n### HIGH\n\n**1. Rejected promises from plugin code crash the host process** — `src/runner/index.ts:49`\n\n`vm.runInNewContext` returns the promise object; nothing awaits it, so the rejection becomes an unhandled rejection at the process level. Verified on plain Node (outside vitest's error trapping):\n\n```\nreported result: {\"success\":true,\"output\":{},...}\nError: plugin failed\nNode.js v20.20.1\nEXIT CODE: 1\n```\n\nA single misbehaving plugin takes down the marketplace, and `installPlugin` reports the install as a **success** first. This is the most serious defect: it defeats the entire purpose of sandboxing. Fix: `await` the result inside the try block so rejections land in the existing catch.\n\n**2. Async plugin code produces garbage output** — `src/runner/index.ts:49`\n\nRelated but independently user-visible. `execute('return Promise.resolve(42);')` yields `output: {}` — an unawaited Promise, serialized as an empty object, with `success: true`. `duration` (0.46ms) measures only synchronous scheduling, not actual work. `RunnerService.execute` returns `Promise<SandboxResult>` in `types.ts:68`, which strongly implies async plugin support. Any real plugin doing async work silently returns nothing.\n\nNote the interaction with **timeout**: `vm`'s `timeout` option only interrupts *synchronous* execution. Once awaiting is added, `executeWithTimeout` needs a separate `Promise.race`/timer for async work, otherwise `return new Promise(() => {})` hangs forever. Worth flagging since the current code passes the timeout test only because that test uses a synchronous `while(true){}`.\n\n**3. `get()` returns prereleases over stable, and `coerce` makes prerelease ordering arbitrary** — `src/registry/index.ts:50-62`\n\n`compareVersionsDesc` runs versions through `semver.coerce`, which **strips prerelease tags**: `coerce('1.0.0-alpha')` → `1.0.0`. Two consequences, both verified:\n\n- Registering `q@1.0.0` and `q@2.0.0-beta.1` makes `get('q')` return `2.0.0-beta.1`. Consumers calling `get()` for \"the current version\" receive an unreleased beta.\n- Registering `z@1.0.0` and `z@1.0.0-alpha` makes both compare equal under `rcompare`, so the tie falls to `b.localeCompare(a)`, which returns `1.0.0-alpha` as highest — the prerelease beats its own stable release.\n\nUse `semver.valid(v)` (strict, preserves prerelease) with `semver.rcompare` and only fall back to `coerce` for genuinely non-semver strings. Consider excluding prereleases from `get()` unless no stable version exists, matching npm's `latest` dist-tag semantics.\n\n---\n\n### MEDIUM\n\n**4. `listVersions` sorts then reverses, yielding ascending order** — `src/registry/index.ts:155-160`\n\n`.sort(compareVersionsDesc).reverse()` produces ascending order (verified: `['1.0.0','2.0.0','10.0.0']`). The function is undocumented as to direction and tests only check membership, so this isn't a failure — but it's the opposite of the neighbouring `get()`'s newest-first convention, and `.sort(desc).reverse()` reads like an accident rather than intent. If ascending is deliberate, sort ascending directly and document it; the double operation invites a future \"fix\" that silently flips the order.\n\n**5. Sandbox context is not isolated — plugins mutate and call into host objects** — `src/runner/index.ts:47`\n\n`{ ...context }` is a shallow copy, so nested objects are shared by reference with the caller. Verified: `run.execute('obj.n = 999;', shared)` mutates the caller's `shared.obj`. Passed-in functions are also directly invokable, and any host function reaching the sandbox is a prototype-chain escape vector back into the host realm. Given `installPlugin` passes a `dependencies` array (`src/index.ts:117`) this is currently benign, but the module is a general-purpose sandbox and the docblock at line 46 claims \"Only the caller-supplied bindings are visible\" — which understates the exposure. Either deep-clone/freeze the context or document that callers must pass only plain serializable data.\n\n**6. Memory tracking is noise, not measurement** — `src/runner/index.ts:57`\n\n`process.memoryUsage().heapUsed` deltas measure the whole process heap, not the sandbox. Five identical `return 1;` runs reported: `188872, 385144, 28536, 286912, 28672` bytes — an order of magnitude of variance for identical trivial work, driven by unrelated allocation and GC timing. A GC between samples yields a negative delta, silently clamped to 0 by `Math.max`. The spec asks for \"memory tracking,\" so the field has to exist; the value as computed is not attributable to the plugin. Either document it explicitly as a coarse process-level estimate, or use `vm.measureMemory()` / an isolate-level metric.\n\n**7. `installPlugin` accepts array `dependencies`, producing a confusing error** — `src/index.ts:67`\n\n`typeof [] === 'object'`, so `dependencies: ['dep-a']` passes validation, then `Object.entries` turns indices into names and the user sees `Invalid version range for \"0\": a`. Add `Array.isArray` (and a null check — `dependencies: null` currently passes validation and is silently coerced to `{}` at line 96, which may be intended but is undocumented).\n\n---\n\n### LOW\n\n**8. `flattenDependencies` drops a self-dependency** — `src/deps/index.ts:134`\n\nThe `current === name` guard is there to keep the root out of its own flattened list, but it also suppresses a genuine `self → self` edge: `flattenDependencies('self')` returns `[]` where `hasCircularDependency('self')` correctly returns `true`. Minor inconsistency between two views of the same graph.\n\n**9. `search` returns `[]` for an empty query** — `src/registry/index.ts:147`\n\nA defensible choice (an empty needle would otherwise match everything via `instr`), but it's an undocumented special case that differs from `list()`. Worth a comment stating the intent.\n\n**10. Non-Error throws stringify poorly** — `src/runner/index.ts:18-23`\n\n`throw {code: 5}` yields `error: \"[object Object]\"`, and `throw new Error()` yields `\"Error\"`. Plugin authors throwing plain objects — common in JS — get an undiagnosable message. Consider JSON-stringifying non-Error throws.\n\n**11. `resolve` crashes on non-array input** — `src/resolver/index.ts:31`\n\n`resolve(null)` throws `Cannot read properties of null (reading 'map')`. Every other entry point in this module guards its inputs (`maxSatisfying` checks `Array.isArray` at line 15), so this is an inconsistency in an otherwise defensively-written module.\n\n**12. Duplicate reviews per user are permitted** — `src/reviews/index.ts:53`\n\nThe same `userId` can review the same plugin repeatedly, each one counting toward the average — a trivial rating-manipulation vector. The spec doesn't require one-review-per-user, so this is a design note rather than a defect; if intentional, document it.\n\n---\n\n### Plan alignment\n\nNo deviations from `TASK.md`. All five modules exist with the required backing technology (`better-sqlite3`, `semver`, node `vm`), `createMarketplace()` implements the six specified `installPlugin` steps in the specified order, and `src/types.ts` is byte-identical (SHA-256 check passes). Build, lint, and all 55 tests are clean.\n\nThe one spec-level gap worth raising with the plan author rather than the implementer: the spec says \"Execute entry point in sandbox\" without stating whether a *failing* sandbox execution should fail the install. The current code returns a successful `InstallResult` with `sandboxResult.success === false` and leaves the plugin registered (verified). That's a reasonable reading, but combined with finding #1 it means a plugin that rejects asynchronously is recorded as installed *and* crashes the process — worth an explicit decision.\n\nFiles reviewed: `/workspace/src/registry/index.ts`, `/workspace/src/resolver/index.ts`, `/workspace/src/runner/index.ts`, `/workspace/src/reviews/index.ts`, `/workspace/src/deps/index.ts`, `/workspace/src/index.ts`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a8640a93074aeea40 (use SendMessage with to: 'a8640a93074aeea40' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 48697\ntool_uses: 23\nduration_ms: 192953</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d6588523-39cb-4758-b12c-c1d9fc9187a8","uuid":"1dd0110a-6cc7-4c99-8f5b-1a20dc664c5f","timestamp":"2026-07-25T01:36:55.711Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Review the implementation in /workspace committed as `3f12e78` (diff against base `c50d2f4`). Run `git diff c50d2f4 HEAD` to see it.\n\nContext: this is a plugin marketplace built to the spec in /workspace/TASK.md. Five independent modules (src/registry, src/resolver, src/runner, src/reviews, src/deps) plus an integration layer src/index.ts. `src/types.ts` is READ-ONLY (SHA-256 verified in tests) and must not be modi